{#
  The README a deployment directory carries: what it holds, then how to run each format it was written in.

  Every section ends with a single newline, and whatever follows one opens with the blank line that separates them.
#}
# {{ name }}

{% if output_files | length == 1 %}
This directory contains the generated `{{ output_files | first }}` for the `{{ name }}` scenario.
{% else %}
This directory contains generated deployment configs for the `{{ name }}` scenario.
{# Broken only between names, so the list fits the line length however many formats it holds. #}
{{ ("Files: " ~ output_files | map("code") | join(", ") ~ ".") | fill }}
{% endif %}

{% if module_names %}
The scenario exposes these workflow modules: {{ module_names | join(", ") }}.
{% else %}
No workflow modules were selected in the scenario input.
{% endif %}

{#
  Where a published scenario's artifacts come from, above the run sections rather than inside one, because it is true
  of every way the scenario starts: the `mise` deployment runs released binaries, and the compose and Kubernetes ones
  run published images. Said once per run mode it would be three copies of one fact, and said inside `mise` alone it
  would read as a property of that mode. A local scenario says nothing here -- building what it runs is the
  unremarkable case, and the run sections already show the builds.
#}
{% if published %}
This scenario is rendered against released artifacts rather than builds of this repository:
the images it names are published, and the binaries it runs are the released ones. Only the
image carrying its own module set is still built locally, since no release can hold a module
set particular to one deployment.

{% endif %}
{#
  The credential file is worth explaining because its absence is silent: `mise` skips an `_.file` it cannot find
  without a warning, and Docker Compose treats a missing `env_file` the same way, so a copy without it starts a
  collector with no root password rather than failing.
#}
{% set credentials = "OpenObserve, OTLP and object store" if shares_hubs else "OpenObserve and OTLP" %}
`{{ secrets_file }}` holds the scenario's derived {{ credentials }} credentials. It is derived from the
scenario input, so regenerating this deployment rewrites it; if it is missing, regenerate before
starting the stack. A deployment generated outside the repository is written with a `.gitignore`
covering it, so its credential is not committed by whatever repository it lands in.

{# Every way of running the tasks needs what they name installed first, so the step comes once, ahead of them all. #}
